@@ -117,6 +117,8 @@ Detection of that violation is where they diverge. `PetController` recognizes it
 
 Open sessions are not held across view rendering (`spring.jpa.open-in-view` is disabled), so associations that a view needs are fetched eagerly at the mapping level.
 
+That disabled setting is also what makes a refused edit leave the stored row alone. Form binding writes the submitted values onto the loaded entity before validation runs, so the in-memory instance carries the rejected input while the form is redisplayed. Nothing reaches the database because the graph is detached, no handler declares a transaction, and the error path returns the view before the single repository `save`. Only the vet list is cached, so no stale copy survives the request either. `VisitControllerIntegrationTests` pins that invariant against the running application. It books a visit and submits a correction the application must refuse. The row is then re-read through `OwnerRepository` in a later transaction. The controller tests cannot reach that far: they stub the repository and observe only that no save was requested.
+
 ## Dependency Policy
 
 Minimize external dependencies. Every dependency is an attack surface and a maintenance burden.
